  "narration": [
    {
      "start": 0.0,
      "end": 7.2,
      "text": "Welcome. In this short lesson, we will learn what happens when an acid meets a base. This reaction is called neutralization."
    },
    {
      "start": 7.8,
      "end": 17.09,
      "text": "Before any experiment, a chemist puts on protective gear: a lab coat, gloves, and safety goggles. Acids and bases can burn the skin and the eyes."
    },
    {
      "start": 17.69,
      "end": 31.28,
      "text": "Here we pour a solution into a beaker. An acid, like hydrochloric acid, releases hydrogen ions in water. A base, like sodium hydroxide, releases hydroxide ions. Combine the two solutions slowly."
    },
    {
      "start": 31.88,
      "end": 45.65,
      "text": "As we stir, each hydrogen ion joins a hydroxide ion to form a molecule of water. The sodium and chloride ions that remain form table salt. So sodium hydroxide plus hydrochloric acid gives sodium chloride and water."
    },
    {
      "start": 46.25,
      "end": 54.85,
      "text": "An indicator tells us when the reaction is complete. When exactly enough base has been added, the solution reaches p H seven, which is neutral."
    },
    {
      "start": 55.45,
      "end": 63.98,
      "text": "Accidents can happen. If a glass container breaks, never pick up the shards with your hands. Tell your teacher, and use a brush and a dustpan."
    },
    {
      "start": 64.58,
      "end": 73.93,
      "text": "To summarize: an acid plus a base gives a salt plus water. This is neutralization, and it is used in everything from antacid tablets to treating acidic soil."
    }
  ],
